	\begin{abstract}
		This paper presents the design of a Road-Side-Unit (RSU) based autonomous intersection
		controller that replaces fixed traffic signals with an on-demand grant scheme for cooperative
		vehicles. Starting from a set of functional and non-functional requirements, the controller is
		specified as a network of UPPAAL timed automata and formally verified against safety and
		reachability queries, including collision freedom and deadlock freedom. The verified model is
		then mapped into a deterministic C baseline and then into a hardware/software codesign proposal.
		The software part keeps scheduling, messaging, and priority logic in FreeRTOS, while the
		repeated conflict checking is proposed as a VHDL safety coprocessor. The system supports both
		M/M/1 and M/M/2 crossing service, emergency pre-emption, V2I/I2V/V2V communication, and
		fairness logic for avoiding starvation.
	\end{abstract}
	
	\begin{IEEEkeywords}
		RSU, hardware/software codesign, FreeRTOS, VHDL, UPPAAL, autonomous intersection, M/M/1, M/M/2,
		emergency priority, formal verification.
	\end{IEEEkeywords}
	
	\section{Introduction}
	
	Autonomous and cooperative vehicles do not need a fixed-cycle traffic light to coordinate an
	intersection. A Road-Side Unit (RSU) can grant crossing permission on demand and react to the
	actual traffic pattern. The central engineering problem is that this dynamic grant scheme must
	remain safe: the RSU has to avoid collisions, resolve priority between emergency and normal
	traffic, and behave predictably enough to be verified.
	
	This paper compares two service disciplines for the crossing zone. The first is a conservative
	M/M/1 model where only one vehicle may occupy the intersection at a time. The second is an M/M/2
	model where two non-conflicting vehicles may cross simultaneously. The project follows a
	model-based path: requirements first, then UPPAAL verification, then a deterministic C baseline,
	and finally a hardware/software codesign proposal using FreeRTOS and a VHDL safety coprocessor.
	
	The contribution of this paper is:
	
	\begin{itemize}
		\item a functional and non-functional requirements specification for the RSU controller;
		\item UPPAAL timed-automata models of the controller and vehicle fleet;
		\item comparison of a single-queue and a four-queue UPPAAL model;
		\item a quantitative comparison of waiting time and crossing throughput for M/M/1 and M/M/2 service;
		\item a deterministic C baseline based on the verified model;
		\item a hardware/software partitioning proposal with FreeRTOS software and VHDL conflict checking;
		\item validation of M/M/1, M/M/2, emergency priority, and safety behaviour.
	\end{itemize}
	
	\section{Related Work}
	\label{sec:related}
	
	Reservation-based control of intersections for autonomous vehicles was popularised by Dresner and
	Stone, whose Autonomous Intersection Management (AIM) scheme lets vehicles reserve space-time slots
	from a central manager instead of obeying fixed signals~\cite{dresner}. Later cooperative
	intersection management approaches, surveyed by Chen and Englund, extend this idea with
	vehicle-to-infrastructure and vehicle-to-vehicle communication and with a range of scheduling and
	priority policies~\cite{chen}. The RSU grant scheme in this paper follows the same reservation-style
	principle: a vehicle may enter the conflict zone only once the infrastructure has granted it.
	
	Most AIM and cooperative-intersection studies evaluate throughput and delay through simulation, but
	comparatively few provide a formal safety guarantee for the admission logic itself. Formal
	verification of such timing-dependent controllers builds on the theory of timed automata introduced
	by Alur and Dill~\cite{alur}, for which the UPPAAL model checker offers an established modelling and
	verification environment~\cite{uppaal}. This work combines the two directions: the reservation-style
	RSU controller is expressed as a network of UPPAAL timed automata and formally checked for collision
	and deadlock freedom before being mapped to a deterministic C baseline and a hardware/software
	codesign proposal. The single-queue and four-queue variants studied here therefore contribute a
	formally verified, per-approach view of the intersection admission problem rather than a purely
	simulation-based one.
	
	\section{Requirements and System Scope}
	
	The system is an RSU-based autonomous intersection controller. Vehicles arrive from North, South,
	East, and West. Normal vehicles communicate with the RSU using V2I messages such as request,
	grant, wait, and done. Emergency vehicles additionally broadcast a V2V emergency beacon to warn
	nearby vehicles. The RSU manages waiting vehicles, checks path conflicts, grants or withholds
	crossing permission, and prevents unsafe simultaneous movement.
	
