	\subsection{UPPAAL Model Variants}
	\label{sec:uppaal_variants}
	
	Two UPPAAL variants were used during modelling. The first variant uses four separate approach
	queues, one queue for each incoming road direction. The second variant uses a simpler single
	waiting structure for the six-vehicle intersection model. Both models keep the same safety idea:
	the RSU may grant a vehicle only when the intersection capacity is not exceeded and no conflicting
	vehicle is already crossing. The comparison is useful because the single-queue model is easier to
	read and verify first, while the four-queue model is closer to the real intersection structure
	where vehicles arrive from East, West, North, and South approaches.
	
	\subsubsection{Four-Queue UPPAAL Model}
	\label{sec:uppaal_four_queue}
	
	The four-queue model represents one queue per approach: East, West, North, and South. Each vehicle
	first sends a V2I request and is inserted into the queue of its origin road. The RSU then considers
	the head vehicle of each approach queue and selects the globally best grantable candidate according
	to emergency priority, main-street priority, turning priority, and the conflict check. The physical
	intersection is still shared: therefore, the four queues do not mean four independent servers. In
	M/M/1 mode only one vehicle can occupy the intersection, while in M/M/2 mode at most two
	non-conflicting vehicles can cross together.
	
