	\subsubsection{Summary and Comparison of the Two Queueing Models}
	\label{sec:uppaal_queue_comparison}
	
	Two variants of the RSU-controlled intersection were modelled and verified in UPPAAL. Both share
	the same underlying safety, priority, and conflict logic, and differ only in how waiting vehicles
	are organised before being granted access to the intersection.
	
	In the single-queue model, all approaching vehicles are managed in one shared priority queue at
	the RSU. The RSU evaluates every waiting vehicle globally and grants access to the highest-priority
	vehicle for which intersection capacity is available. Priority follows emergency, then main road,
	then side road, with right-turn-before-left-turn resolving conflicting manoeuvres and vehicle ID as
	a tie-breaker. This corresponds to a single pooled queueing station, operating either in M/M/1 or
	M/M/2 mode.
	
	In the four-queue model, waiting vehicles are partitioned by their approach into four separate
	queues: East, West, North, and South. This is represented by the array \texttt{queueLen[R]} with
	$R=4$. Service follows a head-of-line discipline: only the highest-priority vehicle of each
	approach, namely its queue head computed by \texttt{headOfQueue}, is eligible for service. The RSU
	then selects the globally best grantable head across the four approaches. This corresponds to a
	per-approach controller whose four queues feed a single shared crossing.
	
	In both models, intersection capacity and conflict detection remain intersection-wide. At most one
	vehicle in M/M/1 mode, or two non-conflicting vehicles in M/M/2 mode, may occupy the crossing
	simultaneously, because the physical intersection is a single shared resource regardless of how the
	waiting vehicles are organised. The four queues therefore share one server; they are not four
	independent servers.
	
	Both models were verified against the same set of core properties, all of which hold: absence of
	deadlock, absence of collisions, respect of the capacity bound \texttt{crossingCount}
	$\leq$ \texttt{MM\_MODE}, mutual exclusion of the critical North-right/South-left manoeuvre, and
	reachability of both the emergency crossing and the simultaneous crossing of two non-conflicting
	main-road vehicles. The four-queue model additionally satisfies per-approach queue-length bounds,
	confirming that each of the four queues behaves correctly.
	
	For the intersection-level safety and priority properties evaluated here, the two models are
	behaviourally equivalent. The four-queue model differs only by enforcing head-of-line ordering
	within each approach and by making the queue structure explicit. The single-queue model yields a
	smaller state space and represents the intersection as one pooled resource, whereas the four-queue
	model more closely mirrors a realistic per-approach or per-lane controller at the cost of a
	slightly larger state space.
	
