	\section{HW/SW Codesign Partition}
	\label{sec:partition}
	
	Because the UPPAAL model and the C baseline already agree, the codesign partition is
	behaviour-preserving by construction. It changes where a function runs, not the verified safety
	contract. The partitioning rule is simple: regular, parallel, and timing-critical work moves to
	hardware, while irregular, control-heavy, and frequently revised work stays in software.
	
	The scheduler loop, V2I/I2V/V2V message handling, state updates, emergency handling, and priority
	logic stay in software. Conflict checking is different: \texttt{conflict(a,b)},
	\texttt{conflictsWithCrossing(i)}, and \texttt{noCollision()} are pure functions of the current
	state. Therefore, they are good candidates for hardware implementation. In hardware, all pairwise
	conflict checks can evaluate in parallel and return a bounded-time \texttt{safe\_to\_grant} result.
	
