	\section{Emergency and Fairness Handling}
	\label{sec:fairness}
	
	While the emergency vehicle V0 is waiting, the controller blocks new grants to normal vehicles and
	broadcasts an I2V yield message. V0 is granted only when the intersection becomes safe. Emergency
	priority therefore changes the order of service, but it does not bypass the safety check.
	
	\begin{figure*}[t]
		\centering
		\includegraphics[trim=0 0 0 0,clip,width=0.78\linewidth]{Emergency_Vehicle_Sequence_Diagram.png}
		\caption{Sequence of an emergency pre-emption. The emergency request and V2V beacon activate
			emergency mode, normal traffic yields, the RSU checks safety, grants the emergency vehicle, and
			then returns to normal scheduling.}
		\label{fig:emerg}
	\end{figure*}
	
	The strict-priority baseline can starve the lowest-priority movement, especially V5. To reduce
	this risk, an aging counter can be used. The longer a vehicle waits, the more its effective
	priority improves. This fairness extension remains in software because it is stateful and
	policy-dependent. It only changes which safe vehicle is selected; it never overrides the hardware
	safety decision.
	
	\section{Hardware Safety Coprocessor and Interface}
	
	The hardware side of the design is a combinational conflict and safety coprocessor. It receives
	the vehicle state, including origin, turn direction, emergency flag, and crossing flag. It checks
	whether any pair of currently crossing vehicles conflicts and whether a candidate vehicle is safe
	to grant.
	
