	\section{Results and Discussion}
	\label{sec:results}
	
	\subsection{M/M/1 Result}
	
	In M/M/1 mode, only one vehicle is granted at a time. The value of \texttt{crossingCount} never
	reaches 2, which confirms single-server behaviour.
	
	\begin{lstlisting}[caption={M/M/1 log excerpt.},label={fig:mm1log}]
		[t= 6] RSU GRANT -> V1 (EAST STRAIGHT) cross=1
		[t= 8] V2 REQ (WEST STRAIGHT) -> RSU  <-- queued
		[t=11] V1 DONE -> Idle cross=0
		[t=12] RSU GRANT -> V2 (WEST STRAIGHT) cross=1
	\end{lstlisting}
	
	\subsection{M/M/2 Result}
	
	In M/M/2 mode, V1 and V2 can be granted together because their movements are non-conflicting.
	The value of \texttt{crossingCount} reaches 2, proving that the second service slot is used safely.
	
	\begin{lstlisting}[caption={M/M/2 log excerpt.},label={fig:mm2log}]
		[t= 6] V1 REQ (EAST STRAIGHT) -> RSU
		[t= 6] RSU GRANT -> V1 (EAST STRAIGHT) cross=1
		[t= 8] V2 REQ (WEST STRAIGHT) -> RSU
		[t= 8] RSU GRANT -> V2 (WEST STRAIGHT) cross=2  <-- M/M/2
		[t=11] V1 DONE -> Idle cross=1
		[t=12] V4 REQ (NORTH RIGHT) -> RSU
		[t=12] V5 REQ (SOUTH LEFT) -> RSU
		[t=14] RSU GRANT -> V4 (NORTH RIGHT) cross=1   <-- V4 only
	\end{lstlisting}
	
	\subsection{Quantitative Comparison of M/M/1 and M/M/2}
	\label{sec:quant}
	
	To quantify the benefit of the second service slot, waiting and crossing-completion times were
	extracted directly from the deterministic run logs of Listings~\ref{fig:mm1log}
	and~\ref{fig:mm2log}, using the arrival periods and crossing times of Table~\ref{tab:fleet}. All
	values are given in the abstract time units of the model. Table~\ref{tab:quant} reports the results
	for the non-conflicting main-road pair V1 and V2, which is the pair eligible for parallel crossing
	in M/M/2 mode. The waiting time of a vehicle is measured from its request to its grant, and the
	makespan is the time at which the last of the two vehicles finishes crossing.
	
	\begin{table}[t]
		\centering
		\footnotesize
		\caption{Quantitative Comparison of M/M/1 and M/M/2 for the Main-Road Pair (V1, V2), in Model Time Units}
		\label{tab:quant}
		\begin{tabularx}{\columnwidth}{@{}lYY@{}}
			\toprule
			\textbf{Metric} & \textbf{M/M/1} & \textbf{M/M/2} \\
			\midrule
			V1 waiting time & 0 & 0 \\
			V2 waiting time & 4 & 0 \\
			Mean waiting time (V1, V2) & 2.0 & 0.0 \\
			Max concurrent crossings & 1 & 2 \\
			Makespan to clear V1 and V2 & 17 & 13 \\
			\bottomrule
		\end{tabularx}
	\end{table}
	
	In M/M/1 mode, V2 must wait for V1 to leave the intersection before it can be granted, giving a
	mean waiting time of 2.0 time units for the pair and a makespan of 17. In M/M/2 mode, V1 and V2 are
	granted in parallel because their straight movements from opposite approaches do not conflict; both
	waiting times fall to zero and the pair clears at $t=13$, a reduction of 4 time units (about
	$24\%$) in makespan. This quantifies the throughput benefit of the pooled second server. Crucially,
	the improvement does not weaken safety: in the same M/M/2 run the critical pair V4 and V5 is still
	correctly serialised, with V4 granted alone at $t=14$ (waiting time 2) and V5 held back, so the
	second slot is used only for non-conflicting movements.
	
	\subsection{Emergency Result}
	
	V0 is queued with highest priority. The RSU broadcasts an I2V yield message, waits until the
	intersection is safe, and then grants V0 exclusively. This confirms emergency pre-emption without
	violating the conflict rules.
	
	\subsection{Safety Result}
	
	Across the tested runs, the collision condition never fails. The model keeps
	\texttt{crossingCount} within the selected mode and never grants the conflicting V4/V5 pair
	together.
	
