	\section{Limitations and Future Work}
	\label{sec:limitations}
	
	The current results are based on the deterministic C baseline and UPPAAL verification. The
	quantitative comparison is drawn from a single illustrative scenario rather than a statistical
	sweep of arrival rates, so the reported waiting times and makespan should be read as indicative of
	the M/M/2 benefit rather than as steady-state averages. The FreeRTOS implementation and VHDL safety
	coprocessor are planned as the next implementation step. Future work includes hardware simulation
	in ModelSim, end-to-end timing measurement, a statistical evaluation of waiting time and throughput
	over randomised arrival patterns, fairness verification with aging, and deployment on FPGA or PCB
	hardware.
	
	\section{Conclusion}
	
	This paper presented an RSU-based autonomous intersection controller from requirements to UPPAAL
	verification, C baseline implementation, and hardware/software codesign proposal. The UPPAAL model
	shows that the system is deadlock-free, collision-free, respects M/M/1 and M/M/2 capacity, supports
	emergency priority, and allows safe parallel crossing for non-conflicting movements. A quantitative
	comparison of the two service modes shows that M/M/2 removes the waiting time of the non-conflicting
	main-road pair and shortens the time to clear it by about $24\%$ in the studied scenario, without
	weakening the conflict guarantees. The comparison between the single-queue and four-queue models
	shows that both preserve the same safety behaviour, while the four-queue model represents a more
	realistic per-approach controller.
	
